//# Helpers for tests.
//#
//# `testing.check(expr)` checks `expr` as `assert expr` does and returns a
//# `Result<Subject, AssertionFailure>` where `assert` would fail the test, so
//# a test can inspect or render the failure. `Clock` names the clock a test
//# group runs under.

import
    assertions.AssertionFailure
    results.Result
end

/// Which clock a `group` runs under, declared as `clock Clock.Virtual`
/// or `clock Clock.System` beside the group's `boot` and `setup`.
///
/// Tests that wait are slow when they pass and unreliable when the machine
/// is busy. A virtual clock jumps forward whenever every task in the test
/// is blocked, so waiting costs nothing and takes the same path every run,
/// and work that can never finish is reported as a deadlock rather than
/// hanging.
///
/// It is not the default, because a virtual clock changes behavior.
/// Waiting on something outside the program (reading input, a real network
/// call) never lets the clock move and is reported as a deadlock.
/// Background work still running when a test ends is an error, where under
/// the real clock it carries into the next test. `Instant.now` starts at
/// midnight on 1 January 2000.
///
/// A group without a `clock` line runs under `System`, so each group that
/// wants virtual time declares it.
pub enum Clock
    /// The real clock. The default.
    System

    /// A clock that only advances when every task in the test is blocked.
    Virtual
end

/// Returns `Ok(subject)` when `subject` would pass `assert`, and otherwise
/// `Err(failure)`, the `AssertionFailure` that `nomi test` would report.
//! assert testing.check(1 == 1)
//! refute testing.check(1 == 2)
//
pub host fn check<T>(subject: T) -> Result<T, AssertionFailure>
